;;; partition-values.slatl — Motoi Scheme Reference · broken-examples
;;;
;;; Kept ON PURPOSE (CLAUDE.md § Broken-examples). A DIVERGENCE: the verb
;;; runs but the CALLER assumes the wrong SHAPE. SRFI-1 `partition` returns
;;; TWO VALUES; code that treats the result as a two-element list (car/cadr)
;;; mis-binds because a `values` package is not a pair.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "broken-example — partition returns TWO values, not a list; car/cadr mis-binds")

(broken-example :name "partition"
  :library "broken-examples"
  :teaches "multiple-values-vs-list-return"
  :std-target "SRFI-1"
  :status "conformed"
  :training-eligible #t
  :provenance "scheme/reference/broken-examples/partition-values.slatl"

  ;; ─── THE BROKEN CODE (runs, wrong assumption) ───
  :broken-code "(let ((p (partition odd? (list 1 2 3 4)))) (display (car p)) (display (cadr p)))"
  :broken-output "(nothing displayed — car of a values-package is not a list element)"
  :runs "yes — but treats TWO values as if they were a single list"

  :why
"SRFI-1: `(partition pred list)` returns TWO VALUES — the elements that
satisfy `pred`, and those that do not — to be consumed with
`call-with-values` / `let-values`:

    (call-with-values (lambda () (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4)))
      (lambda (yes no) ...))   ; yes=(1 3)  no=(2 4)

Motoi's `partition` (lib/std/srfi-1.js) now conforms and returns a real
`values` package. Treating that package as a list — `(car p)` / `(cadr p)`
— fails: `(pair? (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4)))` is `#f`, so `car`/`cadr`
do not observe the two parts. The two values must be received, not indexed."

  :fix-code "(call-with-values (lambda () (partition odd? (list 1 2 3 4))) (lambda (yes no) (display yes) (display no)))"
  :fix-output "(1 3)(2 4)"
  :fix-runs "yes"
  :fix-note
"partition delivers TWO values. Receive them with `call-with-values` or,
more idiomatically, `let-values`:

    (let-values (((yes no) (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4))))
      (list yes no))   ; => ((1 3) (2 4))

`define-values` and `receive` (SRFI-8) are NOT wired in Motoi — reach for
`let-values` or `call-with-values`."

  :lesson
"Return-SHAPE divergence is subtler than a wrong number: the values are
right, the RECEPTION is wrong. SRFI-1 leans on multiple values for
`partition`, `span`, `break`, `car+cdr`. Once a Scheme HAS a `values`
protocol, treating those results as lists is the mistake — the older
list-return spelling is the broken one now. Bind with let-values."

  :three-other-libs (
    (:lib "SRFI-1"
     :status "two values"
     :code "(call-with-values (lambda () (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4))) list) ; => ((1 3) (2 4)) as TWO values"
     :note "The target contract, now honored. Requires call-with-values / let-values to observe.")
    (:lib "Racket"
     :status "two values (partition returns 2 values)"
     :code "(define-values (yes no) (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4)))"
     :note "Racket returns two values, consumed with define-values (not wired in Motoi).")
    (:lib "R6RS (SRFI-1 port)"
     :status "two values"
     :code "(let-values (((yes no) (partition odd? '(1 2 3 4)))) (list yes no))"
     :note "Same two-value contract via let-values — the idiom Motoi uses.")
  )

  :motoi-standard
"Conformed 2026-07-25: SRFI-1 `partition` returns two values via Motoi's
real `values` / `call-with-values` protocol (R7RS §6.10). The prior
single-list return was retired; this card now teaches the receive-with-
let-values idiom and flags list-indexing (car/cadr) as the mistake.")
